WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.120 --> 00:00:13.020
*Dan stares unblinkingly*

00:00:40.740 --> 00:00:45.420
"CATS" ends with Old Deuteronomy, played by Judi Dench, explaining to the audience that,

00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:48.140
now that they have witnessed the Jellicle Ritual, they should require

00:00:48.200 --> 00:00:50.800
no further explanation, no longer require

00:00:50.840 --> 00:00:53.800
an interpreter to the Jellicle Ways.

00:00:54.280 --> 00:00:57.080
Few things could be further from the truth.

00:00:57.580 --> 00:01:01.180
I feel utterly unprepared to talk about Cats on its own terms

00:01:01.180 --> 00:01:04.000
because I don't know these songs. I don't know

00:01:04.000 --> 00:01:10.120
these characters. I'm not familiar with this story and, very clearly, Cats has no interest in

00:01:10.120 --> 00:01:11.760
aiding the uninitiated.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:14.825
There is a character, a stripey white cat

00:01:14.825 --> 00:01:17.055
that is abandoned in a junkyard at the start of the film,

00:01:17.055 --> 00:01:19.865
who fills the space that would normally be occupied by

00:01:19.865 --> 00:01:23.095
an audience surrogate. I think her name is Liz...?

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:26.000
I should and will look it up, but it's fairly incidental and,

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:28.280
unlike most of the other cats, she

00:01:28.285 --> 00:01:32.700
doesn't get an entire musical number that mostly consists of saying her own name repeatedly.

00:01:32.700 --> 00:01:36.000
She was definitely named after a queen...

00:01:36.525 --> 00:01:40.425
She is an outsider to the local culture of the cats who occupy this neighborhood,

00:01:40.425 --> 00:01:43.345
providing an opportunity for the locals to explain the way of things.

00:01:43.345 --> 00:01:45.805
Indeed, these explanations make up the

00:01:45.805 --> 00:01:48.855
bulk of  the musical numbers,  the show largely consisting of a series of

00:01:48.855 --> 00:01:52.295
cats explaining who they are to Liz and the audience.

00:01:52.475 --> 00:01:55.525
The problem is that the explanations, for the most part,

00:01:55.525 --> 00:01:58.800
are all nonsense. This is not accidental,

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:01.480
the show is mostly Seussian nonsense words and

00:02:01.480 --> 00:02:04.260
freeform absurdity, but 109

00:02:04.360 --> 00:02:06.700
minutes of nonsense is, to say the least,

00:02:06.705 --> 00:02:08.685
a little unmooring.

00:02:08.800 --> 00:02:11.420
Maybe that's the joke. Now you understand

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:14.140
the Jellicle Ways of Jellicle Cats, but

00:02:14.145 --> 00:02:16.465
... you don't. And you never will, because

00:02:16.465 --> 00:02:19.280
you can't. You've been Punk'd.

00:02:20.140 --> 00:02:22.980
What I have pieced together is that the cats have convened for

00:02:22.980 --> 00:02:26.100
a ritual where Old Deuteronomy picks a worthy cat who gets

00:02:26.100 --> 00:02:27.400
to be reborn.

00:02:27.960 --> 00:02:29.595
I don't want to interrogate the metaphysics

00:02:29.600 --> 00:02:32.260
of Andrew Lloyd Weber's Cats because canon

00:02:32.300 --> 00:02:35.280
is an abyss deeper than the Uncanny Valley Cats lives in,

00:02:35.340 --> 00:02:38.400
but at some point I *believe* it was mentioned that this

00:02:38.400 --> 00:02:41.455
process, the balloon trip to the Heaviside Layer,

00:02:41.460 --> 00:02:43.620
erases memories, which strikes me as

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:45.900
making this ritual death not much

00:02:45.900 --> 00:02:47.860
different from  regular death.

00:02:48.220 --> 00:02:50.820
Anyway, it turns out a lot of cats are really

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:52.100
eager to die.

00:02:52.505 --> 00:02:54.345
The winner is a cat that everyone hates,

00:02:54.345 --> 00:02:57.195
for reasons I was unable to parse. I guess

00:02:57.195 --> 00:02:59.975
at some point in the past she teamed up with local villain

00:02:59.975 --> 00:03:02.645
Macavity, who was either a prankster or a

00:03:02.645 --> 00:03:06.100
serial killer. The closest thing to an evolving plot consists

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:07.360
of Macavity kidnapping

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:09.740
the various death candidates so that he,

00:03:09.745 --> 00:03:13.065
as the sole remaining candidate, can win by default.

00:03:13.065 --> 00:03:15.245
His plan is foiled and the cat that everyone

00:03:15.245 --> 00:03:20.120
hates wins and gets to die, but, in a good way. What is happening is

00:03:20.120 --> 00:03:22.380
pretty simple, even if why

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:24.360
remains beyond human ken.

00:03:24.465 --> 00:03:27.645
This stuff, though, this is the easy stuff.

00:03:27.680 --> 00:03:31.380
This is just what you get when you sign up to see Cats.

00:03:31.380 --> 00:03:33.645
You're not really there for plot. You're there for

00:03:33.645 --> 00:03:36.980
music and dancing. Dancing and film have an

00:03:36.980 --> 00:03:39.000
odd relationship and it's probably no

00:03:39.000 --> 00:03:41.000
coincidence that the prevalence of dance in

00:03:41.000 --> 00:03:44.700
film has become increasingly niche as cinema has grown more sophisticated.

00:03:45.840 --> 00:03:48.780
The beauty of performance dance is in the physicality,

00:03:48.780 --> 00:03:50.660
that you are watching people

00:03:50.660 --> 00:03:53.380
do something with their bodies at the very edges of

00:03:53.380 --> 00:03:54.660
human performance.

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:57.360
When we live in an age where cinema can effortlessly

00:03:57.580 --> 00:03:58.780
fake performance,

00:03:58.780 --> 00:04:01.720
it gets a lot more difficult to persuade the audience that

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:03.535
what they're seeing is impressive,

00:04:03.535 --> 00:04:05.735
and it becomes altogether impossible when

00:04:05.735 --> 00:04:07.515
every human's body has been

00:04:07.520 --> 00:04:09.560
replaced with a cartoon cat skin.

00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:13.340
And this is well and truly where everything goes off the rails.

00:04:13.340 --> 00:04:16.120
Cats is a film seated so firmly at the bottom

00:04:16.120 --> 00:04:18.960
of the Uncanny Valley that it has set up residences,

00:04:18.960 --> 00:04:21.700
sown and harvested wheat, raised children, and

00:04:21.700 --> 00:04:24.740
developed its own system of divine mathematics.

00:04:24.980 --> 00:04:27.875
In addition to the digital skin, the actual

00:04:27.875 --> 00:04:30.145
physical feats of dancers are coarsely

00:04:30.145 --> 00:04:32.460
blended with total cartoon replacements,

00:04:32.460 --> 00:04:35.940
having the too-precise movements of a Fortnite emote.

00:04:36.440 --> 00:04:38.640
One of the first shots of the film features Robert

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:42.035
Fairchild's human face hovering in front of his cat head like a cardboard

00:04:42.040 --> 00:04:44.680
Halloween mask, an unfinished effect that

00:04:44.680 --> 00:04:46.840
crops up several times in the movie, most

00:04:46.840 --> 00:04:50.000
extensively in a sequence involving singing and dancing mice and

00:04:50.120 --> 00:04:52.200
cockroaches with human faces.

00:04:53.780 --> 00:04:56.920
Rebel Wilson eats several of the cockroaches.

00:04:57.700 --> 00:04:59.600
Speaking of Rebel Wilson, not

00:04:59.600 --> 00:05:01.760
once but twice in the film, she unzips her

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:04.740
digital cat skin and steps out to reveal

00:05:04.740 --> 00:05:06.615
the same cat skin, but now wearing

00:05:06.615 --> 00:05:09.245
a sequin vest and shorts combo, like a

00:05:09.245 --> 00:05:12.520
Vegas cigarette girl from the '70s. The effect

00:05:12.520 --> 00:05:16.220
is so unsettling that I felt my own skin trying to escape.

00:05:16.680 --> 00:05:19.620
Cats has a very odd relationship with clothes.

00:05:19.840 --> 00:05:21.940
Some cats wear them, others don't.

00:05:22.100 --> 00:05:23.460
It's maybe a little weird, but a fairly

00:05:23.465 --> 00:05:26.395
innocuous theatrical convention that is FAR

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:29.540
from the hardest thing in this film to adjust to...

00:05:29.900 --> 00:05:32.760
at least, until those cats take their clothes off.

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:35.595
Idris Elba's Macavity spends the majority of the film

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:38.840
wearing a giant fur coat and it is impossible to describe just how

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:40.240
uncomfortable it is

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:43.440
when he suddenly apparates wearing nothing at all.

00:05:43.740 --> 00:05:46.260
Despite the plethora of cats on screen

00:05:46.265 --> 00:05:49.415
who aren't wearing clothes, the act of disrobing

00:05:49.415 --> 00:05:51.365
shattered the convention.

00:05:51.365 --> 00:05:53.940
It becomes nudity, and a nudity that

00:05:53.940 --> 00:05:56.635
feels so bizarrely unintentional that,

00:05:56.635 --> 00:05:59.035
despite it being deliberately recorded for

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:01.760
all future human generations to see,

00:06:01.900 --> 00:06:04.580
the instinct is to politely look away in

00:06:04.580 --> 00:06:07.720
embarrassment and respect for Mr. Elba's modesty

00:06:07.720 --> 00:06:09.540
as though director Tom Hooper had

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:12.060
somehow managed to sneak up behind him and

00:06:12.060 --> 00:06:14.380
steal his clothes. These moments

00:06:14.380 --> 00:06:17.095
tend to have a knock-on effect that sharply calls attention to

00:06:17.095 --> 00:06:20.075
all the cats on screen and, like an optical illusion,

00:06:20.080 --> 00:06:23.475
a switch is flipped in the brain, and suddenly

00:06:23.760 --> 00:06:26.480
they're ALL naked. A room full

00:06:26.485 --> 00:06:30.280
of naked human cats writhing and yowling.

00:06:31.220 --> 00:06:35.240
The film is permeated with a subtextual horniness that is made

00:06:35.245 --> 00:06:38.025
unsettling by virtue of being subtextual,

00:06:38.025 --> 00:06:39.955
by virtue of a disconnect

00:06:39.960 --> 00:06:43.320
between the subject of the script and the subject of the camera.

00:06:43.880 --> 00:06:45.940
These are not songs about sex

00:06:45.940 --> 00:06:48.320
and seduction, but nevertheless cats

00:06:48.320 --> 00:06:50.640
thrust their Ken-doll-smooth genitals

00:06:50.640 --> 00:06:52.900
at the audience, splay their legs and

00:06:52.900 --> 00:06:55.880
end multiple musical numbers laying on the floor in a

00:06:55.880 --> 00:06:58.140
post-orgasmic heap.

00:06:58.140 --> 00:07:01.360
Everyone who sees Cats will have their own reaction,

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:03.535
their own moment that becomes seared

00:07:03.535 --> 00:07:06.545
into their being for the rest of time like

00:07:06.545 --> 00:07:09.480
prophets of old glimpsing the true face of God.

00:07:09.760 --> 00:07:11.540
I, for one, am unable

00:07:11.540 --> 00:07:14.680
to express in words the scream

00:07:14.680 --> 00:07:17.400
that emanated from deep within my soul

00:07:17.400 --> 00:07:19.680
when Judi Dench's Old Deuteronomy

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:21.800
spreads her legs wide and

00:07:21.800 --> 00:07:24.620
scissors them in the air to express her approval of

00:07:24.620 --> 00:07:26.600
another cat's musical number.

